<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────── §01 EXPOSURE BIAS -->
<section id="s-bias" data-code="intro">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Problem</div>
<h2>Exposure bias: why off-policy KD silently fails</h2>
<p>
  Standard knowledge distillation fine-tunes a student model on sequences generated by the teacher — or on human-written data with the teacher providing soft token-level labels. At training time the student <em>always sees correct, teacher-quality prefixes</em> as context. At inference time it must condition on its <em>own previously generated tokens</em>, including any mistakes it makes along the way.
</p>
<p>
  This mismatch is called <strong>exposure bias</strong> (Ranzato et al., 2015). One wrong token shifts the context distribution out-of-distribution for everything that follows. The model was never trained to recover from its own errors; subsequent predictions are built on an increasingly incorrect foundation. The survey (arXiv:2604.00626) formalises this as O(T²) scaling — the cumulative bias grows with the square of sequence length.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">exposure-bias.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Press Play to watch the error cascade unfold</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="canvas-wrap">
      <canvas id="exp-canvas" height="210"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="canvas-btns">
      <button class="canvas-btn" id="exp-play">▶ Play</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" id="exp-reset">↺ Reset</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The cascade is concrete: the student generates "India" when the correct continuation was "sunny" — now every subsequent token is conditioned on a wrong premise. At inference, there is no teacher to steer back. For short sequences the effect is mild; for chain-of-thought reasoning traces that run thousands of tokens, the compounding is catastrophic.
</p>
<div class="callout">
  <strong>Why it matters now:</strong> Modern reasoning models generate multi-step traces of 1,000–8,000 tokens. Each step's correctness depends on all prior steps. Off-policy distillation's exposure bias is not a subtle statistical quirk — it is a structural failure mode that grows worse the longer your reasoning chain is.
</div>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────── §02 THE FIX -->
<section id="s-fix" data-code="fix">
<div class="section-no">02 — The On-Policy Fix</div>
<h2>Training on the student's own generations</h2>
<p>
  On-policy distillation (OPD) closes the train/inference gap by a single change: the <strong>student generates its own sequences during training</strong>, and the teacher provides feedback — soft logits or a reward — on those student-generated sequences. The training distribution is now π<sub>student</sub> instead of π<sub>teacher</sub>, exactly matching inference.
</p>

