<h3>Three foundational methods</h3>
<p>
  <strong>GKD — Generalized Knowledge Distillation</strong> (arXiv:2306.13649, ICLR 2024) is the canonical reference. It formalises OPD as minimising a chosen f-divergence over student-sampled sequences, and shows seamless integration with RLHF pipelines. The student generates, the teacher provides soft labels, the student updates.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>MiniLLM</strong> (arXiv:2306.08543, ICLR 2024) takes a specific divergence stance: minimise <em>reverse</em> KL(student ‖ teacher) rather than the standard forward KL. Reverse KL requires student samples by definition, making MiniLLM inherently on-policy. It reduces over-diffusion at the cost of slightly reduced output diversity.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>DistiLLM</strong> (arXiv:2402.03898, ICML 2024) introduces <em>skew KL</em> — a parametric blend of forward and reverse KL — and an adaptive off-policy replay that reuses past student rollouts. Result: competitive quality at up to <strong>4.3× lower training cost</strong> vs. pure on-policy methods.
</p>
<p>
  The key insight shared by all three: <strong>off-policy vs. on-policy is a training-data choice, not an objective choice</strong>. The divergence (forward KL, reverse KL, skew KL) is a separate axis. You can use any objective on-policy; which you choose determines mode-seeking vs. mode-covering behaviour.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Lightning OPD</strong> (arXiv:2604.13010) extends this axis with a third option — <em>offline on-policy</em>: sample from the student once during SFT, precompute the teacher's log-probabilities over those rollouts, then reuse them throughout OPD training without a live teacher server. The key theoretical guarantee is the <em>teacher consistency condition</em>: the SFT teacher and the OPD teacher must be the same model. Swapping in a stronger OPD teacher violates the gradient alignment that makes the cached logprobs a valid substitute for online scoring — and degrades results even though the teacher is objectively better. Lightning OPD reduces training cost by <strong>4×</strong> while matching the convergence of fully online OPD.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────── §03 DIVERGENCE ZOO -->
<section id="s-divergence" data-code="divergence">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Divergence Zoo</div>
<h2>Which objective: mode-covering vs. mode-seeking</h2>
<p>
  Given a teacher distribution and an on-policy student, which divergence to minimise is not obvious. The choice determines whether the student <em>covers all teacher modes</em> (broad but sometimes incoherent) or <em>locks onto the strongest mode</em> (precise but potentially narrow). The visualisation below uses a bimodal teacher representing a question with two valid answers — for example "capital of France" answered with "Paris" (dominant) or "Île-de-France" (less common but correct).
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">divergence-objectives.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Select an objective to see how the student distribution aligns</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="canvas-wrap">
      <canvas id="div-canvas" height="200"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="canvas-btns">
      <button class="canvas-btn active" id="div-fkl">Forward KL</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" id="div-rkl">Reverse KL</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" id="div-skew">Skew-KL</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" id="div-todi">Per-token (ToDi)</button>
    </div>
    <div id="div-desc" style="margin-top:.75rem;font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted2);line-height:1.65;min-height:2.5rem"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Objective comparison</div>
  <span class="math-var">Forward KL</span>    KL(π<sub>T</sub> ‖ π<sub>S</sub>) = E<sub>x~π<sub>T</sub></sub>[log π<sub>T</sub> − log π<sub>S</sub>]   <span class="math-comment">← off-policy; mode-covering</span>
  <span class="math-var">Reverse KL</span>   KL(π<sub>S</sub> ‖ π<sub>T</sub>) = E<sub>x~π<sub>S</sub></sub>[log π<sub>S</sub> − log π<sub>T</sub>]   <span class="math-comment">← on-policy; mode-seeking</span>
  <span class="math-var">Skew-KL</span>     (1−α)·KL(π<sub>S</sub> ‖ π<sub>T</sub>) + α·KL(π<sub>T</sub> ‖ π<sub>S</sub>)           <span class="math-comment">← interpolates; α∈[0,1]</span>
  <span class="math-var">ToDi</span>         w(t)·FKL + (1−w(t))·RKL,  w(t) = σ(log π<sub>T</sub>(t)/π<sub>S</sub>(t))   <span class="math-comment">← per-token adaptive</span>
</div>

<h3>ToDi: token-level adaptive weighting</h3>
<p>
  <strong>ToDi</strong> (arXiv:2505.16297, EMNLP 2025 Oral) provides a gradient-level explanation for why neither FKL nor RKL is universally better: at the token level, forward KL <em>boosts underestimated tokens</em> (where student probability is too low) while reverse KL <em>suppresses overestimated tokens</em> (where student is over-confident). A per-token sigmoid weighting based on log π<sub>T</sub>/π<sub>S</sub> selects the right treatment for each token, consistently outperforming either fixed objective.
</p>

<h3>Stabilisation: avoiding diffusion and collapse</h3>
<p>
  The two failure modes of pure FKL and RKL are well-documented. FKL causes <strong>over-diffusion</strong>: the student spreads mass across all teacher modes, producing generic, hedging responses. RKL causes <strong>mode collapse</strong>: the student locks onto a single mode and ignores valid alternatives. The 2026 paper arXiv:2603.07079 proposes <em>entropy-aware OPD</em> that monitors per-step entropy and switches objectives to stay in a healthy operating range.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>"Rethinking On-Policy Distillation"</strong> (arXiv:2604.13016, THUNLP) is the closest the field has to a systematic <em>why-does-OPD-fail</em> guide. It takes a three-part approach: <em>phenomenology</em> (empirically documenting entropy collapse, distribution shift, and learning instability across training runs), <em>mechanism</em> (tracing each failure to its root cause — chiefly the mismatch between the on-policy sampling regime and the gradient signal's implicit assumptions), and <em>recipe</em> (concrete guidelines for on/off-policy mixing ratios and training schedules). Where entropy-aware OPD offers a runtime fix, this paper offers a diagnostic framework: understand which failure mode you are in before choosing an intervention.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>SDPO</strong> (arXiv:2601.20802) sidesteps the FKL/RKL axis entirely by redefining what the reference distribution <em>is</em>. Instead of an external teacher, it uses the model's own <em>feedback-conditioned self</em> as the reference — the same model, but conditioned on a textual description of its error (a runtime exception, a judge critique, or a wrong-answer trace). Distilling from feedback-conditioned predictions back into the unconditioned policy produces dense, token-level supervision that is structurally on-policy and requires no external teacher. In this framing the FKL/RKL choice becomes secondary to the prior question: <em>what does the reference distribution represent?</em>
</p>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────── §04 REASONING -->
<section id="s-reasoning" data-code="reasoning">
<div class="section-no">04 — The Reasoning Frontier</div>
<h2>Where the stakes are highest: long chain-of-thought</h2>
<p>
  Reasoning models generate chains of thought that can run 1,000–8,000 tokens before the final answer. At that length, off-policy distillation's O(T²) exposure bias is not a theoretical concern — it is a wall. Three patterns have emerged for handling it.
</p>

