<h3>Pattern A — STILL: off-policy warm-start, then on-policy refinement</h3>
<p>
  <strong>STILL-2</strong> (arXiv:2412.09413) uses the three-stage pipeline above. Stage 1 is off-policy SFT on long-form "slow thinking" traces from a strong teacher — this provides a stable warm-start without early on-policy instability. Stages 2–3 shift to on-policy generation and iterative self-improvement. <strong>STILL-3</strong> (arXiv:2503.04548) then shows that RL (GRPO) on top of the distilled model yields further gains — reaching <strong>39.33% on AIME 2024</strong> with tool use.
</p>

<h3>Pattern B — rStar-Math: pure on-policy self-evolution</h3>
<p>
  <strong>rStar-Math</strong> (arXiv:2501.04519) needs no external teacher. It runs four rounds of: (1) MCTS rollouts generating step-by-step solutions, (2) a process preference model (PPM) that scores the steps, (3) retraining on the best-scored traces. The model provides its own training signal at every round.
</p>

<table class="results-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>MATH (baseline)</th><th>MATH (after rStar)</th><th>AIME 2024</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr class="highlight-row"><td>Qwen2.5-Math-7B</td><td class="mn">58.8%</td><td class="mn best">90.0%</td><td class="mn">53.3%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Phi3-mini-3.8B</td><td class="mn">41.4%</td><td class="mn best">86.4%</td><td class="mn">—</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Pattern C — DeepSeek-R1: RL first, distill the traces</h3>
<p>
  <strong>DeepSeek-R1</strong> (arXiv:2501.12948) takes the reverse order: train the large model with pure RL (GRPO) until it develops robust reasoning, then distil those RL-generated traces into smaller student models via standard SFT. The distillation step is technically off-policy — but it distils from an on-policy RL distribution, not from human data. DeepSeek-R1-Distill-7B reaches <strong>92.8% on MATH-500</strong> with this approach.
</p>
<p>
  The <strong>cognitive behaviors paper</strong> (arXiv:2503.01307) adds a nuance: whether RL succeeds at all depends on whether the base model already exhibits verification, backtracking, subgoal-setting, and backward chaining. Distilling those <em>reasoning behaviors</em> — even from wrong-answer traces — is a prerequisite for RL to work. This positions on-policy distillation as a necessary first step before RL, not just an alternative to it.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Beyond GRPO and On-Policy Distillation</strong> (arXiv:2605.12483) provides the theoretical framing for why this ordering is optimal: sparse sequence-level rewards (GRPO) are best for building a strong teacher in an exploration-heavy regime, while dense token-level supervision (on-policy distillation) compresses that knowledge into students most efficiently in an exploitation-heavy regime. The two paradigms aren't competing — each is optimal for a different phase, and their correct sequencing (RL to strengthen the teacher, then OPD to transfer) reliably outperforms either alone.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────── §05 DEBATE -->
<section id="s-debate" data-code="debate">
<div class="section-no">05 — The Central Debate</div>
<h2>Does RL actually introduce new capabilities?</h2>
<p>
  The dominant view through 2024 was that RLVR (RL with verifiable rewards) <em>elicits</em> reasoning capabilities that base models and distilled models cannot reach. A NeurIPS 2025 Oral paper (arXiv:2504.13837) challenged this directly with a pass@k sweep.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">pass-at-k.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Click a k button — at high k, base model catches up</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="canvas-wrap">
      <canvas id="passk-canvas" height="240"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="canvas-btns">
      <button class="canvas-btn active" id="pk-1">k = 1</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" id="pk-8">k = 8</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" id="pk-32">k = 32</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" id="pk-128">k = 128</button>
    </div>
    <div id="pk-desc" style="margin-top:.75rem;font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted2);line-height:1.65;min-height:2.5rem"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The finding: at <strong>k = 1</strong>, RLVR models substantially outperform base models — this is what motivates RL training. But at <strong>large k</strong>, the base model's distribution already contains the correct solution; its pass@k converges to the RLVR model's. RLVR reshapes <em>which answers get sampled first</em> but does not add fundamentally new answers. Six different RLVR algorithms showed the same pattern — it is not an artifact of any specific method.
</p>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>The implication for distillation:</strong> If RL only reshapes probability within the base model's existing distribution, but distillation can inject teacher reasoning patterns the student does not have, then on-policy distillation from a stronger teacher may be the only mechanism that genuinely expands student capabilities — not just re-orders them.
</div>

<h3>The CEPO failure mode: answer leakage</h3>
<p>
  Not all on-policy distillation is safe. <strong>CEPO</strong> (arXiv:2605.19436, May 2026) documents a sharp failure mode: when the teacher is conditioned on the correct answer and the student is trained to imitate those teacher logits, the answer information leaks into the gradient. The student learns to "look up" the answer from its context rather than reason to it. In CEPO's experiments, naïve on-policy self-distillation (OPSD, SDPO) <em>fell below the untrained baseline</em>.
</p>
<p>
  CEPO's fix: per-token credit assignment via a contrastive question — does the correct-answer rollout favour this token, <em>and</em> does the wrong-answer rollout disfavour it? Only tokens satisfying both conditions receive weight as genuine reasoning steps. Result: <strong>43.43% vs. GRPO's 41.17%</strong> at 2B scale; <strong>60.56% vs. 57.43%</strong> at 4B scale on competitive math benchmarks.
</p>

<h3>The sparse-to-dense principle: teacher quality matters as much as training regime</h3>
<p>
  <strong>Beyond GRPO and On-Policy Distillation</strong> (arXiv:2605.12483, May 2026) sharpens the debate with a clean empirical hierarchy across the Qwen3 and Llama families: <em>RL-improved-teacher distillation &gt; direct student GRPO &gt; raw-teacher distillation</em>. The finding directly challenges the emerging view that per-student RL is always preferable for small models — a 1.7B student trained via on-policy distillation from an RL-strengthened 8B teacher reaches <strong>79.3% MATH / 25.2% AIME 2024</strong>, versus 75.9% / 19.8% for the same student trained directly with GRPO. Ablations quantify the contributions: removing the RL-improved teacher costs −7.8 MATH points; removing on-policy distillation costs −3.3 points.
</p>
<p>
  The paper frames this as a <em>reward-density allocation principle</em>: sparse sequence-level rewards are best for training a strong teacher (exploration-heavy regime), while dense token-level supervision compresses that knowledge more effectively into small students (exploitation-heavy regime). The two paradigms are complements in a prescribed order — RL first to build a strong teacher, then on-policy distillation to transfer efficiently.
</p>
</section>

